== Chapter XIV

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[
  #set par(justify: false)
  #text(size: 0.9em)[The Power of Sin farther demonstrated by the Effects it hath had in the Lives of Professors. First in actual sins. Secondly in habitual Declensions.]
]

#smallcaps[WE] are now to proceed unto other Evidences of that sad truth which we are in the demonstration of. But the main of our work being past through, I shall be more brief in the management of the Arguments that do remain.

That then which in the next place may be fixed upon, is the demonstration which this Law of sin hath in all Ages given of its power and efficacy, by the woful fruits that it hath brought forth, even in Believers themselves. Now these are of two sorts. First, the great #emph[actual eruptions] of sin in their Lives. Secondly, Their #emph[habitual declensions] from the frames, state and condition of Obedience and Communion with God, which they had obtained, both which by the Rule of #emph[James] before unfolded, are to be laid to the account of this Law of sin, and belong unto the fourth head of its progress, and are both of them convincing evidences of its power and efficacy.

#emph[First,] Consider the fearful #emph[Eruptions] of actual sins that have been in the Lives of Believers, and we shall find our Position evidenced. Should I go through at large with this Consideration, I must recount all the sad and scandalous failings of the Saints that are left on record in the holy Scripture. But the particulars of them are known to all; so that I shall not need to mention them, nor the many aggravations that in their circumstances they are attended with: Only some few things tending to the rendering of our present consideration of them useful, may be remarked. As,

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, They are most of them in the Lives of men that were not of the #emph[lowest form] or ordinary sort of Believers, but of men that had a peculiar #emph[eminency] in them on the account of their walking with God in their Generation. Such were #emph[Noah, Lot, David, Hezekiah,] and others. They were not men of an ordinary size, but higher than their Brethren by the shoulders and upwards in Profession, yea in real Holiness. And surely that must needs be of a mighty efficacy that could hurry such #emph[Giants] in the ways of God into such abominable sins as they fell into. An ordinary Engine could never have turned them out of the course of their obedience. It was a poyson that no #emph[Athletick] constitution of spiritual health, no #emph[Antidote] could withstand.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, And these very men, fell not into their great sins at the #emph[beginning of their profession,] when they had had but little experience of the goodness of God, of the sweetness and pleasantness of Obedience, of the power and craft of sin, of its impulsions, solicitations and surprizals, but after a long course of walking with God, and acquaintance with all these things; together with innumerable motives unto #emph[watchfulness. Noah] according to the lives of men in those days of the World, had walked uprightly with God some #emph[hundreds of years] before he was so surprised as he was, #emph[Gen.] 9. Righteous #emph[Lot] seems to have been towards the end of his days, ere he defiled himself with the abominations recorded. #emph[David] in a short Life, had as much experience of Grace and Sin, and as much close spiritual Communion with God as ever had any of the Sons of men, before he was cast to the ground by this Law of sin. So was it with #emph[Hezekiah] in his degree, which was none of the meanest. Now to set upon such persons, so well acquainted with its power and deceit, so armed and provided against it, that had been conquerours over it for so many years, and to prevail against them, it argues a power and efficacy too #emph[mighty] for every thing but the spirit of the #emph[Almighty] to withstand. Who can look to have a greater stock of #emph[Inherent Grace] than those men had; to have more experience of God, and the excellency of his ways, the sweetness of his love, and of communion with him than they had; who hath either better furniture to oppose sin withal, or more obligation so to do, than they? And yet we see how fearfully they were prevailed against.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Thirdly, As if God had permitted their Falls on set purpose, that we might learn to be wary of this powerful enemy, they all of them fell out when they had #emph[newly received great & stupendious mercies] from the hand of God, that ought to have been strong Obligations unto diligence and watchfulness in close obedience. #emph[Noah] was but newly come forth of that #emph[world of waters] wherein he saw the ungodly world perishing for their sins, and himself preserved by that astonishable Miracle which all Ages must admire: Whilest the Worlds desolation, was an hourly remembrancer unto him of his strange preservation by the immediate care and hand of God, he falls into #emph[Drunkenness. Lot] had newly seen that which every one that thinks on cannot but tremble. He saw, as one speaks, #emph[Hell coming out of Heaven] upon unclean sinners, the greatest evidence, except the Cross of Christ; that God ever gave in his Providence of the Judgment to come. He saw himself and Children delivered by the special care and miraculous hand of God; and yet whilest these strange mercies were fresh upon him, he fell into #emph[Drunkenness and Incest. David] was delivered out of all his troubles and had the necks of his Enemies given him round about, and he makes use of his Peace from a world of tryals and troubles to contrive #emph[Murder and Adultery.] Immediately, it was, after #emph[Hezekiahs] great and miraculos deliverance that he falls into his #emph[carnal pride and boasting.] I say, their falls in such seasons, seem to be permitted on set purpose, to instruct us all in the Truth that we have in hand; so that no persons, in no seasons, with what furniture of Grace soever, can promise themselves security from its prevalency, any other ways, than by keeping close constantly to him, who hath supplies to give out that are above its reach and efficacy. Methinks this should make us look about us. Are we better than #emph[Noah,] who had that testimony from God, that he was a #emph[perfect man in his Generation, and walked with God?] Are we better than #emph[Lot,] whose Righteous Soul was vexed with the evil deeds of ungodly men, and is thereof commended by the Holy Ghost? Are we more holy, wise, and watchful than #emph[David,] who obtained this testimony, that he was #emph[a man after Gods own heart?] Or better than #emph[Hezekiah,] who appealed to God himself, that he had served him uprightly with a perfect heart? And yet what prevalency this Law of sin wrought in and over them, we see. And there is no end of the like examples; they are all set up as #emph[Buoys] to discover unto us the Sands, the Shelves, the Rocks, whereupon they made their shipwrack, to their hazard, danger, loss, yea and would have done to their ruin, had not God been pleased in his faithfulness graciously to prevent it. And this is the first part of this Evidence of the power of sin, from its effects.]]

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, It manifests its #emph[power] in the #emph[habitual declensions] from zeal & holiness, from the frame, state and condition of Obedience and Communion with God, whereunto they had attained, which are found in many Believers. #emph[Promises of growth] and improvement are many, and precious; the #emph[Means] excellent and effectual; the #emph[benefits] great and unspeakable: Yet it often falls out, that instead hereof, decays and declensions are found upon Professors, yea in and upon many of the Saints of God. Now whereas this must needs principally and chiefly be from the strength and efficacy of indwelling sin, and is therefore a great evidence thereof; I shall first evince the Observation it self to be true; namely, That some of the #emph[Saints themselves] do oftentimes so decline from that growth & pimrovement in faith, grace, & holiness which might justly be expected from them; and then show that the cause of this evil lies in that, that we are treating of. And that it is the cause of #emph[total Apostasy] in unsound Professors, shall be after declared. But this is a greater work which we have in hand. The prevailing upon #emph[true Believers] unto a sinful declension & #emph[gradual Apostasy,] requires a putting forth of more strength and efficacy, than the prevailing upon #emph[unsound Professors] unto #emph[total Apostasy.] As the wind which will blow down a #emph[dead tree] that hath no root, to the ground, will scarcely shake or bow a living #emph[well-rooted tree.] But this it will do. There is mention made in the Scripture of the First ways of #emph[David,] and they are commended above #emph[his later,] 2 #emph[Chron.] 17. 3. The #emph[last ways] even of #emph[David] were tainted with the power of #emph[In-dwelling Sin.] Though we have mention only of the actual eruption of sin, yet that uncleanness, and #emph[pride] which was working in him in his Numbering of the People; were certainly rooted in a #emph[declension] from his first frame. Those rushes did not grow without mire. #emph[David] would not have done so in his #emph[younger days,] when he followed God in the wilderness of temptations and tryals, full of faith, love, humility, brokenness of heart, zeal, tender affection unto all the Ordinances of God, all which were eminent in him. But his strength is impaired by the efficacy and deceitfulness of sin; his #emph[Locks] cut, and he becomes a prey to vile Lusts and Temptations. We have a notable Instance in most of the Churches that our Saviour awakens to the consideration of their condition, in the Revelations: We may single out one of them: Many good things there were in the Church of #emph[Ephesus, Chap.] 2. 2, 3. For which it is greatly commended; but yet it is charged with a #emph[decay,] a #emph[declension,] a gradual falling off and Apostasy, #emph[ver.] 4, 5. #emph[Thou hast left thy first Love, remember therefore whence thou art fallen, and do thy first works.] There was a decay both #emph[Inward] in the frame of heart, as to faith, and love, and #emph[outward] as to obedience and works, in comparison of what they had formerly, by the testimony of Christ himself. The same also might be showed concerning the rest of those #emph[Churches,] only one or two of them excepted. #emph[Five] of them are charged with decays and declensions. Hence there is mention in the Scripture of the #emph[Kindness of Youth,] of the #emph[Love of Espousals,] with great commendation, #emph[Jer.] 2. 2, 3. Of our #emph[first Faith,] 1 #emph[Tim.] 5. 12. Of the #emph[beginning of our confidence, Heb.] 3. 14. And cautions are given, that we #emph[lose not the things that we have wrought,] 2 #emph[John] 8. But what need we look back or search for Instances to confirm the truth of this Observation? An #emph[habitual declension] from first Ingagements unto God, from first Attainments of Communion with God, from first strictness in Duties of Obedience, is ordinary and common amongst Professors. Might we to this purpose take a general view of the Professors in these Nations, among whom the Lot of the best of us will be found in part or in whole, in somewhat or in all, to fall, we might be plentifully convinced of the truth of this Observation. Is their #emph[Zeal] for God as warm, living, vigorous, effectual, solicitous, as it was in their first giving themselves unto God? Or rather, is there not a common, slight, selfish frame of spirit in the room of it come upon most Professors? Iniquity hath abounded, and their love hath waxed cold. Was it not of old a burthen to their spirits to hear the Name, and ways, and Worship of God blasphemed and profaned? Could they not have said with the Psalmist, #emph[Ps.] 119. 136. #emph[Rivers of water run down our eyes because men keep not thy Law?] Were not their souls solicitous about the Interest of Christ in the World, like #emph[Eli]'s about the Ark? Did they not contend earnestly for the #emph[Truth once delivered to the Saints,] and every parcel of it? Especially wherein the grace of God, and the glory of the Gospel was especially concerned: Did they not labour to judge and condemn the World by an #emph[holy] and #emph[separate] conversation? And do now the generality of Professors abide in this frame? Have they grown, and made improvement in it? Or is there not a coldness and indifferency grown upon the spirits of many in this thing? Yea, do not many despise all these things, and look upon their own former Zeal, as folly? May we not see many who have formerly been of esteem in ways of Profession, become daily a scorn and reproach through their miscarriages, and that justly, to the men of the World? Is it not with them as it was of old with the Daughters of #emph[Sion, (Isa.] 3. 24.) when God judged them for their sins and wantonness? Hath not the World, and #emph[self] utterly ruined their Profession? And are they not regardless of the things wherein they have formerly declared a singular concernment? Yea, are not #emph[some] come partly on one pretense, partly on another, to an open #emph[enmity] unto, and hatred of the ways of God? They please them no more, but are evil in their eyes. But not to mention such #emph[open Apostates] any farther, whose Hypocrisy the Lord Jesus Christ will shortly judge; how is it with the best? Are not almost all men grown cold and slack as to these things? Are they not less concerned in them than formerly? Are they not grown weary, selfish in their Religion, and so things be #emph[indifferent well] at home, scarce care how thy go abroad in the world? At least do they not prefer their ease, credit, safety, secular advantages before these things? A frame that Christ abhors, and declares, that those in whom it prevails are none of his: Some indeed seem to retain a good zeal for Truth, but wherein they make the fairest appearance, therein will they be found to be most abominable: They cry out against #emph[Errors,] not for Truth, but for Parties and Interests sake. Let a man be on their #emph[Party,] and promote their Interest, be he never so #emph[corrupt] in his Judgment, he is embraced, and it may be admired. This is not #emph[zeal for God,] but for a mans self. It is not #emph[the Zeal of thine House hath eaten me up,] but, #emph[Master, forbid them because they follow not with us.] Better it were doubtless for men never to pretend unto any #emph[Zeal] at all, than to substitute such warthful selfishness in the room of it.]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, Is mens #emph[delight] in the Ordinances & Worship of God the same as in former days? Do they find the same sweetness and #emph[relish] in them as they have done of old? How #emph[precious] hath the #emph[Word] been to them formerly? What joy and delight have they had in attendance thereon? How would they have run and gone to have been made partakers of it, where it was dispenced in its power and purity, in the #emph[evidence and demonstration] of the Spirit? Did they not call the #emph[Sabbath] their delight, and was not the approach of it a real joy unto their Souls? Did they not long after the converse and communion of Saints? And could they not undergo manifold perils for the attainment of it? And doth this frame still abide upon them? Are there not decays and declensions to be found amongst them? May it not be said, #emph[Gray hairs are here and there upon them, and they perceive it not?] Yea, are not men ready to say with them of old, #emph[What a weariness is it? Mal.] 1. 3. It is even a #emph[burthen and a weariness] to be tied up to the observation of all these Ordinances. What need we be all out so strict in the observation of the #emph[Sabbath?] What need we hear so often? What need this distinction in hearing? Insensibly a great disrespect, yea, even a contempt of the pleasant and excellent ways of Christ and his Gospel, is fallen upon many Professors.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Thirdly, May not the same conviction be farther carried on, by an enquiry into the #emph[universal course of obedience,] and the performance of duties that men have been engaged in. Is there the same #emph[conscientious tenderness of sinning] abiding in many as was in days of old? The same exact performance of #emph[private duties?] The same love to the brethren? The same readiness for the Cross? The same humility of mind and spirit? The same self-denial? The steam of mens lusts, wherewith the air is tainted, will not suffer us so to say.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[We need then go no farther than this wretched Generation wherein we live, to evince the truth of the observation laid down, as the foundation of the instance insisted on, the Lord give repentance before it be too late.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Now all these #emph[declensions,] all these decays that are found in some Professors, they all proceed from this root and cause, they are all the product of #emph[Indwelling sin,] and all evince the exceeding power and efficacy of it. For the proof whereof I shall not need to go farther than the #emph[general rule] which out of #emph[James] we have already considered; namely that #emph[lust] or indwelling sin, is the cause of all #emph[actual] sin, and all habitual declensions in Believers. This is that which the Apostle intends in that place to teach and declare. I shall therefore handle these two things, and show, First, That this doth evince a great #emph[efficacy and power] in sin. Secondly, Declare the ways and means whereby it #emph[brings forth] or brings about this cursed effect; all in design of our general end, in calling upon and cautioning Believers to avoid it, to oppose it.]]

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, It appears to be a work of great #emph[power and efficacy,] from the provision that is made against it, which it prevails over. There is in the Covenant of grace plentiful provision made, not only for the preventing of #emph[declensions] and decays in Believers, but also for their continual carrying on towards perfection. As,]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, The #emph[Word] it self, and all the Ordinances of the Gospel, are appointed and given unto us for this end, #emph[Eph.] 4. 11, 12, 13, 14, 15. That which is the end of giving #emph[Gospel Officers] to the Church is the end also of giving all the #emph[Ordinances] to be administered by them. For they are given #emph[for the work of the ministry,] that is, for the administration of the Ordinances of the Gospel. Now what is, or what are these ends? They are all for the preventing of decays and #emph[declensions] in the Saints, all for the carrying them onto Perfection, so it is said, #emph[ver.] 12. In general it is #emph[for the perfecting of the Saints,] carrying on the work of grace in them, and the work of holiness and obedience by them; or for #emph[the edifying of the body of Christ,] their building up in an-encrease of faith and love, even of every true member of the mystical body. But how far are they appointed thus to carry them on, thus to build them up? Hath it bounds fixed to its work? Doth it carry them so far, and then leave them? No, saith the Apostle, #emph[verse] 13. The dispensation of the Word of the Gospel, and the Ordinances thereof, is designed for our help, assistance, and furtherance, until the whole work of faith and obedience is consummate. It is appointed to #emph[perfect] and #emph[complete] that faith, knowledge, and growth in grace and holiness, which is allotted unto us in this world. But what and if oppositions and temptations do lie in the way, #emph[Satan] and his instruments working with great subtilty and deceit? Why #emph[verse] 14. These #emph[Ordinances] are designed for our safe-guarding and deliverance from all their attempts and assaults, that so being preserved in the use of them, #emph[or speaking the truth in love, we may grow up unto him in all things who is the head, even Christ Jesus.] This is in general the use of all #emph[Gospel Ordinances,] the chief and man end for which they were #emph[given] and appointed of God; namely to preserve Believers from all #emph[decays] of faith and obedience, and to carry them on still towards #emph[perfection.] These are means which God, the #emph[good Husbandman,] makes use of, to cause the #emph[Vine] to thrive and bring forth fruit. And I could also manifest the same to be the especial end of them distinctly. Briefly, the #emph[Word] is #emph[milk] and #emph[strong meat,] for the nourishing and strengthning of all sorts, and all degrees of Believers. It hath both #emph[seed] and water in it, and manuring with it, to make them fruitful. The Ordinance of the #emph[Supper] is appointed on purpose for the #emph[strengthning] of our faith in the remembrance of the death of the Lord, and the exercise of love one towards another. The #emph[communion of Saints] is for the edifying each other in faith, love, and obedience.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, There is that which adds weight to this consideration. God suffers us not to be #emph[unmindful] of this assistance he hath afforded us, but is continually #emph[calling upon us] to make use of the means oppointed for the attaining of the end proposed. He shows them unto us, as the #emph[Angel] showed the water-spring to #emph[Hagar] Commands, Exhortations, Promises, Threatenings, are multiplied to this purpose; see them summed up, #emph[Heb.] 2. 1. He is continually saying to us, Why will you die? Why will you #emph[wither and decay?] Come to the #emph[Pastures] provided for you, and your souls shall live. If we see a #emph[Lamb] run from the fold into the Wilderness, we wonder not if it be torn and rent of wild beasts: If we see a #emph[Sheep] leaving its #emph[green Pastures] and water-courses, to abide in dry barren #emph[Heaths,] we count it no marvel, nor enquire farther, if we see him lean and ready to perish. But if we find #emph[Lambs] wounded in the #emph[fold,] we wonder at the boldness and rage of the #emph[beasts of prey,] that durst set upon them there. If we see #emph[Sheep] pining in #emph[full pastures,] we judge them to be diseased and unsound. It is indeed no marvel that poor creatures, who for sake their #emph[own mercies,] and run away from the pasture and fold of Christ in his Ordinances, are rent and torn with divers #emph[lusts,] and do pine away with hunger and famine. But to see men living under, and enjoying all the means of spiritual #emph[thriving,] yet to #emph[decay,] not to be fat and flourishing, but rather daily to pine and wither, this argues some secret powerful distemper, whose poisonous and noxious qualities hinder the virtue and efficacy of the means they enjoy. This is #emph[Indwelling-sin.] So wonderfully powerful, so effectually #emph[poisonous] it is, that it can bring #emph[leanness] on the souls of men in the midst of all precious means of growth and flourishing. It may well make us tremble to see men living under, and in the use of the means of the Gospel, preaching, praying, administration of Sacrameats, and yet grow colder every day than other in zeal for God, more selfish and worldly, even #emph[habitually, to decline] as to the degrees of holiness which they had attained unto.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Thirdly, Together with the dispensation of the #emph[outward means] of spiritual growth or improvement, there are also supplies of grace continually afforded the #emph[Saints] from their Head, Christ Jesus. He is the Head of all the Saints. And he is a #emph[living Head,] and so a living Head, as that he tells us, that #emph[because he liveth, we shall live also, Joh.] 14. 19. He communicates of #emph[spiritual life] to all that are his. In him is the fountain of our life, which is therefore said to be #emph[hid with him in God, Col.] 3. 3. And this life he gives unto his Saints, by #emph[quickening] of them by his Spirit, #emph[Rom.] 9. 11. And he continues it unto them, by the supplies of #emph[living grace] which he communicates unto them. From these #emph[two,] his #emph[quickening] of us, and continually giving out supplies #emph[of life unto us,] he is said to live in us, #emph[Gal.] 2. 20. #emph[I live, yet not I, but Christ liveth in me.] The #emph[spiritual life] which I have is not mine own, not from my self was it educed, not by my self is it maintained, but it is meerly and solely the work of Christ; so that it is not #emph[I that live, but he lives in me,] the whole of my life being from him alone. Neither doth this #emph[living head] communicate only a #emph[bare life] unto believers, that they should meerly #emph[live] & no more a poor, weak, dying life, as it were, but he gives out sufficiently to afford them a strong, vigorous, thriving, flourishing life, #emph[Joh.] 10. 10. He comes not only that his sheep may have life, but that #emph[they may have it more abundantly;] that is, in a plentiful manner, so as that they may flourish, be fat and fruitful Thus is it with the whole body of Christ and every member thereof, #emph[Eph.] 4. 15, 16. #emph[Whereby it grows up into him in all things, which is the Head, even Christ; from whom the whole body fitly joyned together, and compacted, by that which every joynt supplieth, according to the effectual working in the measure of every part, maketh increase of the body unto the edifying of it self in love.] The end of all communications of grace, and supplies of life from this living and blessed Head, is the increase of the whole body, and every member of it, and the edifying of it self in love. His treasures of grace are unsearchable, his stores inexhaustible. His #emph[life,] the fonutain of ours, full and eternal, his #emph[heart] bounteous and large, his #emph[hand] open and liberal; so that there is no doubt but that he communicates supplies of grace for their increase in holiness #emph[abundantly] unto all his Saints. Whence then is it that they do not all flourish and #emph[thrive] accordingly? As you may see it oftentimes in a natural body, so is it here. Though the seat and rise of the blood and spirits in #emph[head] and #emph[heart] be excellently good and sound, yet there may be a #emph[withering member] in the body; somewhat intercepts the influences of life unto it. So that though the #emph[heart] and #emph[head] do perform their office, in giving of #emph[supplies] no less to that, than they do to any other member, yet all the effect produced, is meerly to keep it from #emph[utter perishing;] it grows weak and decaies every day. The withering and decaying of any member in Christs #emph[mystical body,] is not for the want of his communication of grace for an #emph[abundant life,] but from the powerful #emph[interception] that is made of the efficacy of it, by the interposition and opposition of #emph[In-dwelling Sin.] Hence it is that where #emph[Lust] grows strong, a great deal of Grace will but keep the Soul alive, and not give it any eminency in fruitfulness at all. Oftentimes Christ gives very #emph[much Grace,] where not #emph[many] of its effects do appear. It spends its strength, and power, in withstanding the continual assaults of violent corruptions and lusts, so that it cannot put forth its proper virtue towards farther fruitfulness. As a #emph[virtuous Medicine,] that is fit both to check vitious and noxious humours, and to comfort, refresh and strengthen Nature, if the evil humour be strong and greatly prevailing, spends its whole strength and virtue in the subduing and correcting of it, contributing much less to the relief of Nature than otherwise it would do, if it met not with such oppositions: So is it with #emph[the eye-salve,] and the healing grace which we have #emph[abundantly] from the #emph[wings of the Sun of Righteousness.] It is forced oftentimes, to put forth its #emph[virtue] to oppose and contend against, and in any measure subdue prevailing lusts and corruptions: That the Soul receiveth not that #emph[strengthening] unto duties and fruitfulness which otherwise it might receive by it, is from hence. How sound, healthy, and flourishing, how fruitful and exemplary in holiness, might many a soul be, by and with that grace which is continually communicated to it from Christ, which now by reason of the power of in-dwelling sin is only #emph[not dead,] but weak, withering and useless. And this, if any thing, is a notable evidence of the efficacy of #emph[in-dwelling sin,] that it is #emph[able] to give such a stop, and check to the mighty and effectual power of Grace, so that notwithstanding the blessed and continual supplies that we receive from our Head, yet many Believers do #emph[decline] and decay, and that #emph[habitually,] as to what they had attained unto; their #emph[last ways] not answering their #emph[first.] This makes the Vineyard in the very #emph[fruitful hill] to bring forth so many wild Grapes. This makes so many trees barren in fertile fields.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Fourthly, Besides the #emph[continual supplies of Grace] that constantly according to the tenure of the #emph[Covenant] are communicated unto Believers, which keeps them that they thirst no more as to #emph[a total indigence,] there is moreover #emph[a readiness] in the Lord Christ to yield peculiar succour to the souls of his, according as their occasions shall require. The Apostle tells us, that he is #emph[a merciful High-Priest] and #emph[able,] (that is #emph[ready] prepared and willing) #emph[to succour them that are tempted,] Heb. 2. 18. And we are on that account, invited to come #emph[with boldness] to the Throne of Grace, #emph[that we may obtain mercy, and find grace to help in time of need;] that is, #emph[grace] sufficient, seasonable, suitable unto any special tryal or Temptation that we may be exercised withal. Our merciful High-Priest is ready to give out this especial seasonable Grace, over and above those constant Communications of supplies of the Spirit, which we mentioned before. Besides the #emph[never-failing Springs] of ordinary Covenant grace, he hath also peculiar refreshing showers for times of drought. And this is exceedingly to the advantage of the Saints, for their preservation and growth in grace; and there may very many more of the like nature be added. But now I say notwithstanding all these, and the residue of the like importance, such is the power and efficacy of #emph[indwelling-Sin,] so great its #emph[deceitfulness] and restlessness, so many its wiles and #emph[temptations,] it often falls out that many of them for whose growth and improvement all this #emph[provision] is made, do yet as was showed, go back and #emph[decline,] even as to their course of walking with God. #emph[Sampsons] strength fully evidenced it self when he brake #emph[seven new Withs,] and #emph[seven new Cords,] wherewith he was bound, as #emph[burning tow, and as thred.] The noxious humour in the body which is so stubborn, as that no use of the most soveraign remedies can prevail against it, ought to be regarded. Such is this #emph[indwelling-sin] if not watched over: It breaks all the cords made to bind it; it blunts the instruments appointed to root it up; it resists all healing Medicines though never so soveraign, and is therefore assuredly of exceeding efficacy. Besides, Believers have #emph[innumerable Obligations] upon them from the Love, the Command of God, to #emph[grow in grace,] to press forward towards perfection, as they have #emph[abundant means] provided for them so to do. Their doing so, is a matter of the greatest advantage, profit, sweetness, contentment unto them in the world: It is the burden, the trouble of their Souls, that they do not so do, that they are not more holy, more zealous useful, fruitful; they desire it above Life it self; they know it is their duty #emph[to watch] against this Enemy, to fight against it, to pray against it, and so they do. They more desire his #emph[destruction,] than the enjoyment of all this world, and all that it can afford. And yet notwithstanding all this such is the subtilty, and fraud, and violence, and fury, and urgency, and importunity of this Adversary, that it frequently prevails to bring them into the woful condition mentioned. Hence it is with Believers sometimes as it is with men in some places at Sea; they have a good and fair #emph[gale of wind,] it may be all night long; they ply their #emph[tackling,] attend diligently their business, and it may be take great contentment to consider how they proceed in their Voyage: In the morning, or after a season coming to measure what way they have made, and what progress they have had, they find that they are much #emph[backward] of what they were, instead of getting one step #emph[forward;] falling into a swift tide, or current against them, it hath #emph[frustrated] all their Labours, and rendered the wind in their Sails almost useless; somewhat thereby they have born up against the stream, but have made no progress. So is it with Believers: They have a good gale of #emph[supplies] of the Spirit from above, they attend #emph[duties] diligently, pray constantly, hear attentively, and omit nothing that may carry them in their #emph[Voyage] towards Eternity. But after a while, coming seriously to consider by the examination of their hearts and ways, what #emph[progress] they have made, they find that all their assistance, and duties, have not been able to bear them up against some #emph[strong tide] or current of #emph[Indwelling-Sin.] It hath kept them indeed that they have not been driven and split on #emph[rocks] and #emph[shelves;] it hath preserved them from gross, scandalous sins, but yet they have lost in their #emph[spiritual frame,] or gone backwards, and are intangled under many woful decays; which is a #emph[notable] evidence of the #emph[Life of Sin,] about which we are treating. Now because the end of our discovering #emph[this power of Sin,] is, that we may be careful to obviate and prevent it in its #emph[operation,] and because of all the effects that it produceth there is none more dangerous or pernicious than that we have last insisted on, namely, that it prevails upon many #emph[Professors] unto an #emph[habitual declension] from their former ways and Attainments, notwithstanding all the sweetness and excellency which their Souls have found in them, I shall, as was said, in the next place consider by what #emph[Ways and Means,] and through what assistance it usually prevails in this kind, that we may the better be instructed to watch against it.]]
